{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789380579,"nonce":"vkfyviv1hmgcxPDSgaA4Ej5fDVo=","title":"Typed compiler diagnostics a frontend can rephrase","message":"A diagnostic the compiler raises today is a string, built where the\ncondition is found and printed as is. A Python programmer then reads\nabout SSA values, HIR types, blocks and lowering when what happened is\n\"this variable can be None here\" or \"this call passes a str where an\nint is expected\". The frontend cannot improve on the message because\nit never sees the condition, only the text.\n\nMake each diagnostic a value: an enum of kinds with typed fields (the\nsymbol involved, the two types that disagree, the span, the callee and\nargument position, the effect that has no handler), and a default\nrendering in the compiler's own language-neutral terms. A frontend\nregisters a renderer for the kinds it knows how to say better, in its\nown vocabulary and with its own source spans, and falls through to the\ndefault for the rest. The kind, not the text, is what tests assert on,\nso a reworded message breaks nothing.\n\nWhere the compiler already has the pieces:\n\n- `CompilerError` variants are the coarse kinds; each `format!` into\n  one is a candidate fine kind.\n- The Python frontend already maps a runtime condition to its own\n  wording (AttributeError on a null read, TypeError from an unboxer);\n  compile-time diagnostics should reach it the same way.\n- Spans: the compiler's diagnostics carry HIR spans; the frontend owns\n  the mapping back to source lines, so the renderer takes the span and\n  the frontend resolves it.\n\nConstraint from the project rules: nothing in `crates/compiler/`\nmentions a frontend's syntax; the default rendering stays in IR and\neffect terms, and the language-specific wording lives in the frontend\ncrate that registers it.","files":null}]}